A||||6||Ken Wittlief||best car to buy if replacing the drive train?||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||04-16-2001||07:34 PM||||If you are planning on buying a Fiero and upgrading the drivetrain are you better off getting a car with the 4 clyinder engine, since you will be replacing it anyway?<P>Are there any 'real' differences between the base model and the formulas or GTs, besides the drivetrain, that are significant? (besides the body style) Im getting the impression that a base model '88 might be the better way to go.<P>(I already know about the front end and brakes in the '88 vs the earlier years).<P>Also does the wing on the tail of the car do anything besides look cool and make wind noise? I've heard that tail wings and whale tails dont really provide any handling improvements until you get up around 90mph.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-16-2001||08:00 PM||||Wings on Fieros are for looks. There aren't too many places where you can safely drive over 70 in upstate NY, except along 490 toward LeRoy.  I don't hear any wind noise from my wing.  The Borla exhaust rumble covers up most all the wind noise except for the passenger door air leak I hear, although the pssengers don't.  Then you turn on the stereo and you don't hear any outside noise at all.<P>If you are swapping engines, I don't think it makes much difference in Fiero model you buy.  With any larger engine swap, you are more than likly to need to upgrade the suspension anyway, cause its thirteen years old.  Of course the 88 is the most expensive to upgrade, because of the limited production numbers of the 88 Fiero and available replacement parts.||||reg||3
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||04-16-2001||08:07 PM||||Ditto on 88 bits.  However I believe there are enough differences between 4 and v6 to justify a v6 base car.  Fuel pump and wiring, etc.  Depends of course on what drivetrain you want to put in.<BR>Gary||||reg||12
Z||000003||gixxer||04-16-2001||08:08 PM||||I agree with Lar. <BR>But would tend to focus on the '88s for a V8 swap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||04-16-2001||09:37 PM||||Well the only thing that I could think of is that the 4 bangers gnerally speaking have weaker springs, than the V6's like in the GT's and Formulas.  Now if you put in a V8 you are probably going to lower the car and then this makes no difference.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||04-17-2001||12:50 AM||||There are a few differences between a base coupe and a GT or Formula model. #1 and probably the most important is the lack of a rear sway bar in coupes.  If you are going to do a v-8 conversion, make sure you get one!  #2  There is probably a fair amount of weight difference between a loaded GT and a stripped down coupe.  How much?  I really don't know.  #3 Although I'm not sure about this one, but i think the base coupes have different spring rates.  <P>These differences are all primarily in the handling department.  As far as differences in the motor setup of things, I'm not sure about.  Although the 4cly, has the Isuzu 5-speed while the v-6's have the Getrag.  I believe the Getrags are able to handle a fair more amount of torque.  <P>That's about all the differences I can think of.  I don't know how major you think they are, but these are things to consider.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||04-17-2001||01:46 AM||||The 88 suspension is better but you have about 2" more room side to side for a SBC V-8 in the 84 thru 87 Fiero's. 88's have the strut towers angled in more. So depending on the engine it may be easier. You can get the 84-87 cars to handle as well or even better than a stock 88 But cost not counted, the 88 is a better design. As for 4-cyl to V-6, Suspension is stiffer on the V-6, Usually in the springs. Sway bar is thicker on SE/GT/Formula. 88 GT's and Formula's are the only cars with stock front and rear sway bars. Brakes are better on 88's. Parts are less expensive on 84 thru 87. Your best bet is to find a car where the motor died but everything else is in good shape. If you are planning on a 3.4L upgrade get an 85 thru 88 V-6 car. It's almost a drop in swap and the 84's have different harness locations. The difference is minor but still a pain to go to a 2.8/3.1/3.4. If you are doing a 3800 V-6 swap, I hear the GT fastback (86 thru 88) is slightly better due to the slightly higher decklid. Notchbacks have a hump but fastbacks are slightly higher across the entire deck. This has something to do with the location of the alternator. As for Northstar V-8's I am not sure which if any body style is better. Small Block Chevy's have the strut tower issue as I mentioned above. For SBC V-8's you can check out V-8 Archie's site. For all others do a search for what you want. Many swaps are in progress or have been completed. Phil has a very sharp Yellow Formula with a 3800 supercharged, Batboy has a silver V-8 coupe, There are a few Northstars here too. <BR>It basically comes down to what engine you are going to swap in and how much you want to spend.||||reg||1
